00:00:07.817 --> 00:00:09.088
- I actually grew up
in the Virgin Islands,

00:00:09.088 --> 00:00:10.648
so I went to school in St. Thomas,

00:00:10.648 --> 00:00:14.488
and in high school, good hair
was anything that was fine.

00:00:14.488 --> 00:00:16.688
Curly texture is something
that was manageable,

00:00:16.688 --> 00:00:19.819
bouncy, straight,
something that was easy to

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:21.808
maintain, that was considered good hair.

00:00:21.808 --> 00:00:24.088
- Growing up in Atlanta,

00:00:24.088 --> 00:00:25.440
everybody had a relaxer.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.779
I don't remember seeing
anybody that had natural hair.

00:00:28.779 --> 00:00:31.259
There were a lot of kids
that, you know, wore braids

00:00:31.259 --> 00:00:33.491
and things like that,
but I still believe that

00:00:33.491 --> 00:00:37.229
the relaxing process
happened before the braids.

00:00:37.229 --> 00:00:38.598
- I always kept it really short.

00:00:38.598 --> 00:00:40.867
And, I would have friends that said

00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:42.178
it felt like pubic hair.

00:00:42.178 --> 00:00:44.611
For a long time I thought
my hair was really ugly.

00:00:44.611 --> 00:00:47.419
- When I was young, for
a guy, good hair was

00:00:47.419 --> 00:00:49.429
a low cut, you know, even,

00:00:49.429 --> 00:00:51.518
Caesar, number one, maybe you had

00:00:51.518 --> 00:00:53.709
the part with the Nike sign on the back.

00:00:53.709 --> 00:00:55.800
A little bit of flavor,
but pretty much everyone

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.278
had a low cut for the most part.

00:00:57.278 --> 00:00:59.440
- Everyone had straight
hair, that was just the look.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:01.389
Even if that wasn't their natural hair,

00:01:01.389 --> 00:01:04.120
your hair, if it took you
five hours to straighten it,

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.069
you were gonna straighten it, because

00:01:06.069 --> 00:01:08.250
that's what was acceptable.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.683
- I grew up being into, like, punk rock,

00:01:10.683 --> 00:01:12.048
and the skateboarding scene.

00:01:12.048 --> 00:01:13.683
If you look at all the
cool skaters, and you look

00:01:13.683 --> 00:01:15.341
at those hairstyles at the time,

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:17.101
it was all spikes, Mohawks,

00:01:17.101 --> 00:01:19.841
fauxhawks, and my hair could never do it.

00:01:19.841 --> 00:01:20.731
I kept it buzzed.

00:01:20.731 --> 00:01:22.523
Everyone would say, every
time I would get a haircut

00:01:22.523 --> 00:01:24.050
is like, why are you
cutting these curls off?

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:25.371
And I was just like, I can't stand them.

00:01:25.371 --> 00:01:28.392
- My hair history can be best
characterized by control.

00:01:28.392 --> 00:01:30.421
Trying to gel it down,

00:01:30.421 --> 00:01:32.651
brush it down, put it into braids.

00:01:32.651 --> 00:01:34.992
- My mom is black, and my dad is white,

00:01:34.992 --> 00:01:37.443
and my mom had a really demanding job and,

00:01:37.443 --> 00:01:40.171
would be gone by the time
I'd wake up most days.

00:01:40.171 --> 00:01:43.691
And, so it would be left to
my dad, taking care of my hair

00:01:43.691 --> 00:01:45.461
in the morning, and it was a total wreck.

00:01:45.461 --> 00:01:48.032
I remember going to school
with so much hair mayonnaise

00:01:48.032 --> 00:01:49.931
in my hair that it looked gray.

00:01:49.931 --> 00:01:51.931
- Growing up, good hair, and

00:01:51.931 --> 00:01:54.950
what I thought good hair was,
and what I was also taught,

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:56.803
from I guess, from, a little
bit of my surroundings,

00:01:56.803 --> 00:01:58.301
my family and some friends,

00:01:58.301 --> 00:02:00.811
was that good hair was when
you permed your hair, you know.

00:02:00.811 --> 00:02:03.483
So, when I was growing up, I
did perm my hair a few times.

00:02:03.483 --> 00:02:05.741
- Growing up my hair was really short.

00:02:05.741 --> 00:02:07.701
It was like, low on the
top, skin on the side.

00:02:07.701 --> 00:02:08.941
That's the way my mom liked it.

00:02:08.941 --> 00:02:11.530
She said it was like, neat, clean.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.871
- In our society, I think
hair represents a way

00:02:13.871 --> 00:02:15.411
to express your beauty.

00:02:15.411 --> 00:02:19.491
Individually, it can be a way
that you express who you are,

00:02:19.491 --> 00:02:21.749
and how you're feeling that
day, but it's also a way

00:02:21.749 --> 00:02:23.851
in our society, to kind of control

00:02:23.851 --> 00:02:25.301
your aspects of beauty, and

00:02:25.301 --> 00:02:27.320
I was always told
growing up that your hair

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:28.521
was supposed to be straight.

00:02:28.521 --> 00:02:30.209
I didn't have that perfect hair,

00:02:30.209 --> 00:02:32.811
therefore I was othered by society, just,

00:02:32.811 --> 00:02:33.962
with the hair that I have.

00:02:33.962 --> 00:02:35.801
- Perfect hair, good hair,

00:02:35.801 --> 00:02:37.621
when I was younger came from,

00:02:37.621 --> 00:02:41.010
when I used to watch TV,
or magazines or films,

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:42.368
all of the hair would
be long and straight.

00:02:42.368 --> 00:02:44.192
And, even when I was
looking at black women,

00:02:44.192 --> 00:02:48.192
on TV, they would always have
relaxed hair, or long hair.

00:02:48.192 --> 00:02:50.571
They'd be light-skinned, beautiful women,

00:02:50.571 --> 00:02:52.421
with equally beautiful hair

00:02:52.421 --> 00:02:54.643
that was only beautiful
because it was straight.

00:02:54.643 --> 00:02:57.221
- My father, he, growing up, and seeing

00:02:57.221 --> 00:02:58.683
what he's seen, I mean,

00:02:58.683 --> 00:03:00.843
I feel like there's just
this culture there that

00:03:00.843 --> 00:03:03.192
it's like you have to
be groomed, and kept,

00:03:03.192 --> 00:03:07.061
and you can't let what,
pretty much is you grow out.

00:03:07.061 --> 00:03:08.992
Because, I don't know, it's seen as,

00:03:08.992 --> 00:03:09.889
almost wild.

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:12.400
- It was reinforced through
the images we saw on TV.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:16.160
It was reinforced through
the popular people in school,

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.021
the people guys wanted to date.

00:03:18.021 --> 00:03:20.352
You know, you saw that
manifest itself in many ways,

00:03:20.352 --> 00:03:23.000
and, I think today it still rears

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.661
its ugly head in many cases.

00:03:24.661 --> 00:03:26.480
- I feel like straight hair
is only considered good hair,

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:27.781
because that's all you see.

00:03:27.781 --> 00:03:30.931
It's all you see on ads, it's
all you see on billboards,

00:03:30.931 --> 00:03:32.831
it's all you see on television.

00:03:32.831 --> 00:03:34.661
You don't see, really
people that look like me,

00:03:34.661 --> 00:03:36.360
and when you do, their hair's straight.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:39.281
- Trying to date with
natural hair was definitely

00:03:39.281 --> 00:03:42.649
a challenge, because I would
get the guy that was like,

00:03:42.649 --> 00:03:44.913
oh, I'd love your hair
if it was straighter,

00:03:44.913 --> 00:03:46.862
or you'd be more beautiful
if your hair was straight.

00:03:46.862 --> 00:03:50.211
Or if I wear my Afro
just like, washed and go,

00:03:50.211 --> 00:03:51.353
they'd be like, what is that?

00:03:51.353 --> 00:03:53.390
So I think there's a lot
of different elements that

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:57.433
make us feel less secure
or less confident about

00:03:57.433 --> 00:03:58.902
wearing our natural hair.

00:03:58.902 --> 00:04:01.391
- People have made assumptions
by looking at my hair

00:04:01.391 --> 00:04:03.281
that I smoke, I'm a Rasta,

00:04:03.281 --> 00:04:04.529
I listen to Bob Marley.

00:04:04.529 --> 00:04:06.561
Like, those assumptions are frustrating.

00:04:06.561 --> 00:04:08.971
- I committed to having longer hair

00:04:08.971 --> 00:04:11.553
right around the time the
Trayvon Martin case happened.

00:04:11.553 --> 00:04:13.461
When the verdict came out,
I was just kind of like,

00:04:13.461 --> 00:04:15.079
it really doesn't matter, like,

00:04:15.079 --> 00:04:17.931
how acceptable you look to someone else,

00:04:17.931 --> 00:04:19.542
so you should just look however you want,

00:04:19.542 --> 00:04:20.622
what makes you feel comfortable.

00:04:20.622 --> 00:04:23.251
And I think there was a strong
push for just, natural hair.

00:04:23.251 --> 00:04:25.287
I really wanted to like,
embrace that, and embrace

00:04:25.287 --> 00:04:27.026
the natural curls of my hair.

00:04:27.026 --> 00:04:29.146
- I definitely think that
part of the reason I went back

00:04:29.146 --> 00:04:30.807
to curly was the natural hair movement,

00:04:30.807 --> 00:04:33.197
because when I Japanese
straightened my hair,

00:04:33.197 --> 00:04:35.447
all that was really at
my disposal was gels,

00:04:35.447 --> 00:04:37.367
and mousses, and things of that nature,

00:04:37.367 --> 00:04:39.557
which, I felt, was causing
a lot of thinning for me,

00:04:39.557 --> 00:04:42.605
and, didn't really give me,
like, the look that I wanted.

00:04:42.605 --> 00:04:44.736
Whereas now, there are
pomades, there are leave-in

00:04:44.736 --> 00:04:47.824
treatments, there are sea salt sprays,

00:04:47.824 --> 00:04:50.586
like there are so many different
things that I can use now.

00:04:50.586 --> 00:04:52.904
- I have a 16-month old daughter, and,

00:04:52.904 --> 00:04:55.124
her hair is identical to mine, and,

00:04:55.124 --> 00:04:58.136
I tell her, her dad tells her
how beautiful her hair is.

00:04:58.136 --> 00:05:00.117
It's thick, and it's full,

00:05:00.117 --> 00:05:01.866
and it's long for her age.

00:05:01.866 --> 00:05:04.637
It's important that she
sees a reflection of herself

00:05:04.637 --> 00:05:07.485
in me, so, that is another reason why,

00:05:07.485 --> 00:05:10.037
you know, I'm really
adamant, and will continue

00:05:10.037 --> 00:05:12.616
to stay natural, because
I want her to see herself

00:05:12.616 --> 00:05:14.317
in me, and others around her.

00:05:14.317 --> 00:05:16.596
I have friends and family
members who went natural

00:05:16.596 --> 00:05:18.864
after having kids, just for that example.

00:05:18.864 --> 00:05:21.763
And, I want to make sure that
I'm showing and expressing

00:05:21.763 --> 00:05:23.976
and illustrating that,
natural hair is beautiful.

00:05:23.976 --> 00:05:26.157
- I think my hair

00:05:26.157 --> 00:05:29.186
has taught me, in this society and world,

00:05:29.186 --> 00:05:30.925
to just be unapologetic.

00:05:30.925 --> 00:05:32.786
People are gonna love it,

00:05:32.786 --> 00:05:34.655
and then people are gonna fucking hate it.

00:05:34.655 --> 00:05:37.736
People are gonna see you as
unprofessional sometimes,

00:05:37.736 --> 00:05:39.885
and people are gonna have all these ideas

00:05:39.885 --> 00:05:41.706
in their heads about whether, and,

00:05:41.706 --> 00:05:43.173
like what kind of person you are.

00:05:43.173 --> 00:05:46.853
- I feel like my hair
has taught me about the

00:05:46.853 --> 00:05:49.205
importance of confidence
and individuality.

00:05:49.205 --> 00:05:52.213
I fell like black women's
hair is political,

00:05:52.213 --> 00:05:54.725
but is also not, in that,

00:05:54.725 --> 00:05:55.805
it will always be,

00:05:55.805 --> 00:05:57.794
in certain professional settings,

00:05:57.794 --> 00:05:59.874
if you have big in Afro,

00:05:59.874 --> 00:06:01.752
that then may be a problem,

00:06:01.752 --> 00:06:03.803
or, that you'll go into certain spaces,

00:06:03.803 --> 00:06:05.333
and people will try and touch your hair,

00:06:05.333 --> 00:06:07.412
will say something
derogatory about your hair.

00:06:07.412 --> 00:06:09.354
- I often think that my hair

00:06:09.354 --> 00:06:11.485
came out of a lot of

00:06:11.485 --> 00:06:12.853
unwanted struggle.

00:06:12.853 --> 00:06:14.301
Like that sense that,

00:06:14.301 --> 00:06:16.024
nobody loves you, you're useless,

00:06:16.024 --> 00:06:18.504
you don't have any contribute, and like,

00:06:18.504 --> 00:06:21.051
my hair is kind of like,
that reclaiming of like,

00:06:21.051 --> 00:06:23.744
nah girl, like, I'm here to take up space,

00:06:23.744 --> 00:06:25.813
you're gonna look at me, like it or not,

00:06:25.813 --> 00:06:27.264
and most of you are gonna love it.

00:06:27.264 --> 00:06:30.474
- I think in America, and
living in our society,

00:06:30.474 --> 00:06:32.554
a lot of people tell you what is normal

00:06:32.554 --> 00:06:33.464
for your hair.

00:06:33.464 --> 00:06:36.544
In the eighties, you wanted to
have big, frizzy, curly hair.

00:06:36.544 --> 00:06:39.123
Trends change with your
hair, and the only way to,

00:06:39.123 --> 00:06:40.554
I think to truly be happy with your hair,

00:06:40.554 --> 00:06:41.904
is to embrace what works for you,

00:06:41.904 --> 00:06:43.370
to find what works for you.

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:46.325
And for me, my hair represented who I was,

00:06:46.325 --> 00:06:48.213
and my personality, and as I grew older,

00:06:48.213 --> 00:06:49.733
I got more comfortable with my body,

00:06:49.733 --> 00:06:52.093
and got more comfortable
with who I was as a person,

00:06:52.093 --> 00:06:53.525
and I wanted my hair to reflect that.

00:06:53.525 --> 00:06:54.644
- Your hair is beautiful.

00:06:54.644 --> 00:06:56.564
I've lived the straight hair life.

00:06:56.564 --> 00:06:59.314
It was cool, but, I think that the way

00:06:59.314 --> 00:07:01.322
that you are naturally will always

00:07:01.322 --> 00:07:02.594
be more beautiful.

00:07:02.594 --> 00:07:05.514
I think you shine more when
you're your natural self.

00:07:05.514 --> 00:07:09.264
- My hair just taught me to
embrace myself, to love me,

00:07:09.264 --> 00:07:11.423
the way that I came, the
way that I was created,

00:07:11.423 --> 00:07:14.224
and, the way that I am,
right here, raw and all.

00:07:14.224 --> 00:07:16.634
It's not bad to appreciate other cultures,

00:07:16.634 --> 00:07:18.504
but, it's also good to embrace my own.

00:07:18.504 --> 00:07:19.845
And that's what my hair has taught me.

00:07:19.845 --> 00:07:22.502
- I'm just so happy that girls nowadays

00:07:22.502 --> 00:07:25.504
have different kinds of
role models to look up to.

00:07:25.504 --> 00:07:27.384
And I think it's also important that

00:07:27.384 --> 00:07:28.624
you can be versatile.

00:07:28.624 --> 00:07:31.234
One of the fun things I
love about my hair is that,

00:07:31.234 --> 00:07:33.464
tomorrow, I could wake up being like,

00:07:33.464 --> 00:07:35.434
I really want it to be straight.

00:07:35.434 --> 00:07:37.205
And, I can make it straight.

00:07:37.205 --> 00:07:40.864
So, it doesn't have to be any
one thing from day to day,

00:07:40.864 --> 00:07:43.024
year to year, or throughout your life.

00:07:43.024 --> 00:07:45.552
People are constantly
changing and evolving,

00:07:45.552 --> 00:07:47.073
and, we're allowed to do that.

00:07:47.073 --> 00:07:48.453
- I would say find your tribe.

00:07:48.453 --> 00:07:51.274
Find your group, and it
wasn't until I found my pack

00:07:51.274 --> 00:07:54.024
of strong, independent people
that embraced their curls

00:07:54.024 --> 00:07:56.064
that I felt like, oh, I
could run with these people,

00:07:56.064 --> 00:07:56.897
because they are me.

00:07:56.897 --> 00:07:59.693
- I think now, good hair
is whatever you want.

00:07:59.693 --> 00:08:01.885
Whatever makes you feel
the best when you walk

00:08:01.885 --> 00:08:03.324
out the house, is good hair.

00:08:03.324 --> 00:08:04.903
For me, this is good hair for me.

00:08:04.903 --> 00:08:06.313
And for someone else
it might be a low cut.

00:08:06.313 --> 00:08:08.424
Whatever, when you walk out
the house, you feel like

00:08:08.424 --> 00:08:10.874
you're on point, you feel like
your confidence is booming.

00:08:10.874 --> 00:08:11.707
That's good hair.

00:08:11.707 --> 00:08:14.290
(upbeat music)

00:08:21.674 --> 00:08:22.909
(whoosh)

00:08:22.909 --> 00:08:24.025
(hinges creaking)

